## Semantic hint injection — TableSpot (WebTestBench_0010)

**115 hinted elements**, 0 errors / 0 warnings / 0 info under `validate-semtags.mjs --strict`.

### Screens and components annotated

| Screen | Files |
|---|---|
| Shared chrome (all pages) | `components/Header.tsx` |
| Home / restaurant browse | `pages/Index.tsx`, `components/FilterBar.tsx`, `components/RestaurantCard.tsx` |
| Restaurant detail (info, reviews tab, write-review tab, booking, confirmation, not-found) | `pages/RestaurantDetail.tsx`, `components/ReviewCard.tsx`, `components/ReviewForm.tsx`, `components/ReservationForm.tsx` |
| My Reservations (upcoming/past tabs, modify + cancel dialogs) | `pages/MyReservations.tsx`, `components/ReservationCard.tsx` |
| 404 | `pages/NotFound.tsx` |

11 files touched. No shadcn primitive needed prop-widening — `input`, `textarea`, `button`, `select` and `tabs` already `forwardRef` and spread `{...props}`, so the one extra code change the skill permits was unnecessary.

### Id namespaces

`nav.*` · `home.*` · `filters.*` · `restaurants.*` · `restaurant.*` · `reservation.*` · `review.*` · `reservations.*` · `notfound.*`

### Collections — all six fold

`restaurants.grid` [6] · `restaurant.features` [3] · `restaurant.reviews` [2] · `review.form.rating` [5] · `reservations.upcoming` [2] · `reservations.past` [2]. No collection failed to fold.

Two §8 collision risks were real and handled: the desktop and mobile navs render **simultaneously** (desktop is CSS-hidden, not unmounted), so mobile links carry a `.mobile` suffix with identical `data-semtag-state`; and `ReservationCard` renders in both lists, so `collectionId` is a **required** prop — TypeScript enforces it at every call site and the ids stay visible to the source-text linter.

### Judgement calls

- **The date picker is `role="input"`, not `select"`.** The validator warned `select-without-options`. Enumerating a 60-day calendar into `data-semtag-options` would bloat every snapshot — directly against this fork's token-efficiency aim. As an `input` the extractor still reads the trigger's live text, `action="pick-date"` signals click-not-type, and `controls="reservation.date.calendar"` names the popover.
- **Footer links got `role="navigation"` with no `target`.** They don't actually navigate; supplying a target would describe what the UI *should* do, which is test intent.
- Deliberately unhinted: `components/ui/*` primitives, decorative icons, and purely presentational wrappers.

### Verification beyond the linter

The linter reads source text, so I checked the rest in a live browser: a Playwright run over **18 screen states** (seeded with 4 reservations so every list has ≥2 items) confirmed page-wide id uniqueness, valid roles, real DOM ancestry of items under collections, template-literal ids staying unique at runtime, and all 12 `controls` targets resolving — **all runtime checks passed**.

Two initial failure classes turned out to be my checker's fault, not the hints', and I confirmed each empirically rather than assuming: Radix portals dialog content onto `<body>` outside `#root` (so a modal legitimately has no collection ancestor — its `.modify`/`.cancel` triggers are inside the collection and do fold), and `controls` may name a region that only renders once opened. I also had to open the *second* card's dialogs, not just the first, to actually exercise per-item template-literal ids.

### Project checks

`tsc --noEmit` clean · `npm run build` succeeds · `eslint` clean on all 11 modified files.

Two pre-existing failures, **present before I started** and in files I never touched: 3 eslint errors in `ui/command.tsx`, `ui/textarea.tsx` and `tailwind.config.ts`, and a CSS `@import` ordering warning in `index.css`.

Since `data/` is gitignored, I proved the change was attribute-only by diffing against the pristine copy at `web_applications/WebTestBench_0010`: exactly the 11 intended files differ, and after stripping `data-semtag-*` and normalizing whitespace, 4 are byte-identical to the original while the other 7 differ solely by the sanctioned collection-id / nav-id threading described above.

